@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+# Native Grok session transport
+
+The modded native harness supports an explicit `wire_api = "grok"` transport for
+Grok's documented CLI session proxy. This uses the existing Codex turn and tool
+loop. The removed generic `wire_api = "chat"` remains unsupported.
+
+Configure a trusted account-pinned command using the existing provider-auth
+contract. Its private stdout contains only the bearer token. Public status and login controls
+must not return credentials. For example, after account registration in Wake:
+
+```toml
+[model_providers.grok]
+name = "grok"
+wire_api = "grok"
+base_url = "https://cli-chat-proxy.grok.com/v1"
+
+[model_providers.grok.auth]
+command = "codex-wake"
+args = ["--wake-root", "/absolute/private/wake-root", "grok", "auth", "token", "registered-account"]
+cwd = "/absolute/trusted/directory"
+timeout_ms = 5000
+refresh_interval_ms = 60000
+```
+
+The transport requires command authentication and rejects ambient OpenAI auth,
+API-key/environment fallback, gateway auth, WebSockets and standalone search.
+The command arguments select the registered account; credentials are resolved
+once per HTTP request and only its bearer header is forwarded. The request's
+model is also bound to `x-grok-model-override`, alongside the documented native
+session-auth header. Production requests admit only the HTTPS CLI session proxy;
+loopback destinations support local fixtures. Core rejects inference redirects.
+
+A qualified catalog entry must select direct tools, UnifiedExec and the supported
+freeform apply-patch tool, disable Responses Lite and tool search, and advertise
+only qualified capabilities. Eager MCP function namespaces use existing native
+MCP dispatch. This transport alone does not qualify a catalog, account entitlement
+or any model for operator use. Current OpenAI defaults remain unchanged.
+
+The adapter accepts text messages, native function/custom calls and their known
+text results. It wraps a custom tool's input in a JSON `input` argument and restores
+the original native tool name, namespace and call ID. Opaque reasoning is excluded
+from requests. Unsupported history/content is held for an explicit portable
+continuation rather than silently discarded.
+
+Text streams immediately. Function arguments may arrive fragmented and are
+validated before any completed native tool call is emitted. The `[DONE]` marker,
+consistent finish reason, stable response identity, known tool and unique call ID
+are required before committing calls. Missing usage remains unknown. Malformed or
+incomplete streams and unknown results do not become success. An uncertain POST
+or stream failure is non-retryable; reassignment/recovery requires explicit action.
+Local interruption closes the owned stream and does not assert provider-side
+cancellation or zero billing.
+
+Protocol provenance: the official Grok shell documents session-proxy access at
+[Using auth.json for API access](https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/blob/main/crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/README.md#using-authjson-for-api-access).
+The source fixture checks prove local protocol/tool-loop behavior; real sign-in,
+entitlement, exact package qualification and working-profile activation are separate
+gates.
